Build: (A) one self-contained HTML file (inline CSS and JS only, Chart.js from a CDN allowed) with a modern dark-mode design: header with warehouse name; KPI cards (SKUs, units, stock value, issues found, time saved, accuracy); a stock-level bar chart coloured by status; a searchable stock table; a reorder-alerts panel; an issues panel with severity badges, root cause and fix for each; a QR code gallery using the QR image URLs; an efficiency comparison chart (manual vs AI); and a short 'how to run this demo' footer. (B) A written report in Markdown.

Expected output: Two clearly separated parts. PART 1 — HTML DASHBOARD: a complete, valid, self-contained HTML document from <!DOCTYPE html> to </html>, ready to save and open in a browser, populated only with the real demo data. PART 2 — WRITTEN REPORT (Markdown): executive summary with headline efficiency gains; stock position and reorder needs; each issue with explanation, root cause and fix (or the clean audit confirmation); efficiency metrics with the stated assumptions; recommended next steps. Where an input stage returned nothing, say so in plain words instead of showing numbers.

Days to simulate: ${has(state.simulation_days) ? state.simulation_days : 5}

Sample warehouse setup:
${outputs.create_products_and_qr_codes.raw}

Simulate the warehouse day by day (Day 1, Day 2, ...; use relative day labels and HH:MM times, not calendar dates). Include receiving from suppliers, order picking, internal transfers between locations, and cycle-count scans. Aim for roughly 12-20 events per day. Every event is a QR scan record with: event ID, day, time, operator ID, event type (RECEIVE, PICK, TRANSFER, COUNT), product QR payload, from location, to location, quantity, and the system-recorded quantity where relevant.

Inject 6-10 realistic errors on purpose, spread across days, covering: a duplicate scan (same event scanned twice), a quantity mismatch between a physical count and the system, unexplained shrinkage, an item scanned into the wrong location (misplaced), a pick that drives an item to zero or below (stockout), and one unknown or malformed QR code. Do not mark the errors inside the scan log itself.

Expected output: Two sections: (1) SCAN LOG — the full chronological list of scan events with all fields above; (2) INJECTED ERRORS ANSWER KEY — each injected error with its event ID(s), error type, affected SKU and location, and what actually happened. If the sample setup is missing, empty or contains NO DATA, reply with exactly: NO DATA: sample product setup returned nothing — estimated, remembered or example values are never a substitute.

